# Post-Modification Testing

To ensure code quality and prevent regressions, follow these testing guidelines after making any changes:

## After Every Code Change

Run the following command to verify that your code compiles successfully:

```bash
cargo check --workspace
```

This helps catch compilation errors early before submitting the code for review.

## Before Submitting Code

Before considering a response ready for the user, run the full test suite:

```bash
cargo test --workspace
```

## Testing Requirements

- Every significant change MUST be tested
- Never return code to the user without verifying it passes tests
- If tests fail, fix the issues before submitting the response
- For new functionality, add appropriate tests to cover the new code
- When adding new tests, verify they are being discovered by checking the test output

## Test Validation Checklist
### All Langs
- [ ] New functionality has appropriate test coverage
- [ ] Edge cases and error conditions are properly tested
- [ ] Consider property-based tests for better coverage of edge cases and invariants
- [ ] Code checks successfully (remember testing already builds, so you don't need to build if you have run tests right before) with `cargo check --workspace`
- [ ] All tests pass with `cargo test --workspace`
- [ ] For domain types and property based tests, implement the `Arbitrary` trait to generate comprehensive test cases

And after tests pass, fix formatting:
- [ ]   cargo fix --workspace --allow-dirty
- [ ]   cargo clippy --workspace --fix --allow-dirty

Remember that untested code is likely to contain bugs that will frustrate users and require additional iterations to fix.

Remember: A failing build or test suite is a blocker - do not proceed with additional changes until these are fixed.

Note: The `--allow-dirty` flag is used with `cargo fix` and `cargo clippy --fix` to allow fixes in a working directory with uncommitted changes.

- Add tests for any new functionality you've implemented
- **Prefer property-based testing** whenever applicable (see property-based-testing.mdc rule)
- Update existing tests to accommodate interface changes
- Ensure edge cases are covered in your test suite
- For domain models and validation logic, use property tests to verify constraints
- Follow the test organization guidelines (see rust-test-organization.mdc rule)




